lesson:
```
Console.Write("Minutes? ");
int m = int.Parse(Console.ReadLine());
Console.WriteLine($"{m / 60} h {m % 60} min");
```

- `Console.ReadLine()` gives the typed line as a `string`.
- Division: two ints give whole division (`7 / 2` is `3`); make one a double for decimals (`7 / 2.0` or `(double)a / b`).
- `%` remainder; `Math.Round(x, 2)`, `Math.Ceiling(x)` (round up), `Math.Floor`, `Math.Max`, `Math.Sqrt`, `Math.Pow`.
- Formatting inside interpolation: `{price:F2}` (2 decimals), `{big:N0}` (thousands separators), `{share:P0}` (percent).

Computers in different countries write numbers differently (10,5 vs 10.5). Archie's tests use the "invariant" format (a dot); `double.Parse(text, System.Globalization.CultureInfo.InvariantCulture)` reads dots everywhere.

example.code:
using System;

class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        Console.Write("Minutes? ");
        int m = int.Parse(Console.ReadLine() ?? "0");
        Console.WriteLine($"{m / 60} h {m % 60} min");
        Console.WriteLine($"{7 / 2} vs {7 / 2.0}");
        Console.WriteLine($"{10 / 3.0:F2}");
    }
}

